.TH GUILE\-OPENAPI\-GENERATOR 1 "2026-10-31" "guile-openapi-generator" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
guile\-openapi\-generator \- generate Guile Scheme from an OpenAPI document, and an OpenAPI document from Guile routes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B guile\-openapi\-generator
.RB [ generate ]
.B \-i
.I SPEC
.B \-o
.I DIR
.RI [ options ]
.br
.B guile\-openapi\-generator
.B spec
.B \-i
.I ROUTES
.B \-o
.I FILE
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B guile\-openapi\-generator
reads an OpenAPI 3.x document written in YAML or JSON and writes Guile Scheme
that reads like a person wrote it: records that validate themselves, a
controller with a route table and a dispatcher, and an HTTP client whose
procedures take and return records rather than strings.
.PP
The same idea runs backwards as well.  The
.B spec
command reads the routes of a Guile or Artanis server that already exists and
writes the OpenAPI 3.0 document it never had, which can then be refined by
hand or fed to the
.B generate
command.
.PP
The command word may be left out: an invocation that starts with an option
means
.BR generate ,
because that was the only command the tool had for a while.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B generate
Read a specification and write a tree of Guile modules: models, controllers,
a client and a README, according to the options below.
.TP
.B spec
Read the route declarations of a Guile or Artanis source file and write an
OpenAPI 3.0 document in YAML.  Neither file is loaded and no handler runs; the
source is read as data.
.SH OPTIONS FOR GENERATE
.TP
.BI \-\-input " SPEC" "\fR, \fB\-i " SPEC
The OpenAPI document to read: a path, or an http(s) URL.  One of this and
.B \-\-json\-schema\-file
is required.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " DIR" "\fR, \fB\-o " DIR
The directory to write the generated tree to.  Default: \fIgenerated\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-module " NAME" "\fR, \fB\-m " NAME
The root module name.  Default: the API title, in kebab case.
.TP
.BI \-\-targets " LIST" "\fR, \fB\-t " LIST
A comma-separated list of
.IR models ,
.IR controllers ,
.I client
and
.IR readme .
A target that needs another brings it along: a client or a controller always
brings the models it imports.
.TP
.BI \-\-controllers " BACKEND" "\fR, \fB\-c " BACKEND
.IR vanilla ,
.I artanis
or
.IR none .
Default: \fIvanilla\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-http " BACKEND" "\fR, \fB\-H " BACKEND
.IR guile ,
.I curl
or
.IR none .
Default: \fIguile\fP.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-models
Write no record module.  Useful for a recovered specification that has no
schemas yet.
.TP
.BI \-\-model\-suffix " SUFFIX"
Append
.I SUFFIX
to every generated model name.
.TP
.BI \-\-operation\-id\-transform " REGEX:REPLACEMENT"
Rename operationIds with a Perl-style substitution before they become
procedure names.
.TP
.BI \-\-base\-url " URL"
Override the first server URL of the specification, which is what the
generated client starts from.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " FILE"
Read options from a YAML or JSON file.  A command-line flag overrides the
file, and the file overrides the built-in default.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-contained
Copy the runtime modules next to the generated code and rewrite the generated
module forms to import the copies, so the tree needs nothing but Guile and
guile-json.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-validate
Do not check that the input is an OpenAPI 3.x document before generating.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Omit the banner comment that says what each generated file came from.
.TP
.BI \-\-header\-file " FILE"
Add the contents of
.I FILE
to the banner of every generated file, which is where a licence or a project
disclaimer goes.
.TP
.BI \-\-dependencies " MODE"
.I none
or
.IR guix .
The latter writes a
.I dependencies.scm
manifest for
.BR "guix shell \-m" .
.TP
.BI \-\-json\-schema\-file " FILE"
Read a JSON Schema document instead of an OpenAPI one.  The top-level schema
becomes a component; its definitions are hoisted into
.IR components/schemas .
.I FILE#/pointer
selects a schema nested inside a larger document.
.TP
.BI \-\-json\-schema\-root\-name " NAME"
Name the component generated from the top level of a JSON Schema document.
.TP
.BI \-\-api\-fragment " FILE"
Merge a partial OpenAPI document into the primary one.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-auth " 'NAME: VALUE'"
Send a header on every remote fetch: the input, every fragment, and every
remote reference.  Repeatable, and
.I ${VAR}
is expanded from the environment.
.TP
.BI \-\-resolve\-external " MODE"
.IR none ,
.I targeted
or
.IR aggressive .
The default leaves external references alone; the other two copy what they
reach into
.IR components .
.TP
.B \-\-request\-response\-models
Emit a Request and a Response model set, with the members each direction can
carry.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-read\-only
Drop readOnly properties from the single model set.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-write\-only
Drop writeOnly properties from the single model set.
.TP
.B \-\-x\-extensible\-enums
Treat
.I x\-extensible\-enum
as an enumeration instead of ignoring it.
.TP
.B \-\-fault\-tolerant\-enums
Map an unknown string enumeration value to
.IR unrecognized
instead of failing the record.
.TP
.B \-\-non\-null\-response\-payloads
Raise when a declared success body is missing or null, instead of decoding it
to \fB#f\fP.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version and exit.
.SH OPTIONS FOR SPEC
.TP
.BI \-\-input " FILE" "\fR, \fB\-i " FILE
The Guile or Artanis source file whose routes are read.
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE" "\fR, \fB\-o " FILE
The YAML file to write, or
.B \-
for standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-source " READER" "\fR, \fB\-s " READER
.I guile
or
.IR artanis .
Default: \fIguile\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-title " TEXT" "\fR, \fB\-t " TEXT
The
.I info.title
of the document.  Default: \fIAPI\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-api\-version " VERSION" "\fR, \fB\-V " VERSION
The
.I info.version
of the document.  Default: \fI1.0.0\fP.
.TP
.BI \-\-description " TEXT"
The
.I info.description
of the document.
.TP
.BI \-\-server " URL"
A
.I servers
entry.  Repeatable.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-v
Print the version and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Generate a client, controllers and models from a specification:
.PP
.nf
.RS
guile\-openapi\-generator generate \-i openapi.yaml \-o gen \-m petstore
.RE
.fi
.PP
Ask for an Artanis adapter and a libcurl client:
.PP
.nf
.RS
guile\-openapi\-generator generate \-i openapi.yaml \-o gen \\
    \-m petstore \-c artanis \-H curl
.RE
.fi
.PP
Keep the options with the project in a checked-in file:
.PP
.nf
.RS
guile\-openapi\-generator generate \-\-config openapi\-generator.yaml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Recover the specification of an existing Guile server:
.PP
.nf
.RS
guile\-openapi\-generator spec \-i app/controllers.scm \-o openapi.yaml
.RE
.fi
.PP
Recover the specification of an Artanis application, straight to a terminal:
.PP
.nf
.RS
guile\-openapi\-generator spec \-i app/routes.scm \-s artanis \-o \- | less
.RE
.fi
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.B 0
on success, and
.B 1
on any failure, with the reason on standard error.  A generation run that
wrote nothing, an unknown option value, a source with no routes: all of them
stop with status 1 rather than producing something plausible.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GUILE_LOAD_PATH
Guile's module search path.  The launcher puts the installed library on it
itself, so a run does not depend on this variable; it is what a program that
uses the library directly sets.
.TP
.B GUILE_OPENAPI_GENERATOR_LIBDIR
The directory holding
.IR openapi\-generator.scm .
The launcher looks beside itself first and only needs this for a tree that was
moved out of the layout the packages install.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/guile/site/3.0/openapi\-generator.scm
The public module, \fB(openapi\-generator)\fP.
.TP
.I /usr/share/guile/site/3.0/openapi\-generator/
The rest of the library.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/guile/3.0/site\-ccache/openapi\-generator/
The byte-compiled copies, installed by the packages so that loading a module
does not compile it on first use.  The exact path follows the distribution's
Guile.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
The project's README, which is the manual: a quick start, a complete example
application, the generated code explained, and the whole command line.
.PP
https://codeberg.org/jjba23/guile\-openapi\-generator
